-Each row of Table~\ref{tab:headline} shows two settings of one trained
-model, an accurate one and a fast one. The fast setting beats the
-full-order solver on every problem and mesh; the accurate one beats it on
-every linear problem and on the finer meshes of the nonlinear ones.
-
-\paragraph{Linear problems.}
-On Poisson, the L-shape and heat both settings of a row are solves in the
-span of the ordered bank. The head is less accurate there
-(4.48\,\% against 0.83\,\% on Poisson at $4096^2$, Table~\ref{tab:tunability}): on a linear problem the best solve in the bank is the linear
-one, and it is also the cheapest. Because the reduced query does not visit
-the grid until it returns the field, the speedup grows with the mesh, to
-108$\times$ over CG on Poisson at $4096^2$ and
-$\nHeatWideBatchedAccS\times$ over CN--CG on heat at $2048^2$.
-The accurate error is the same at every mesh because it is the bank's
-projection floor, the best fit of the same-grid solution in the span of
-the frozen bank; the reduced solve reaches that floor (0.83\,\% on
-Poisson), and the floor does not change once the solution is resolved.
-
-\paragraph{Burgers.}
-At coarse meshes the reduced solve is slower than Newton--BiCGStab, but its
-cost barely changes with the mesh. On the 64 held-out test cases the
-accurate setting (the span of 384) reaches 0.87--1.35\,\% (median
-0.03\,\%) at 0.38--10.7$\times$ the speed of Newton--BiCGStab, and the fast
-setting (the span of 96 up to $1024^2$ and of 128 beyond) 3.06--4.25\,\% at
-1.22--27.5$\times$; the fast setting is faster than the FOM at every mesh and
-the accurate one from $1024^2$.
-
-In three dimensions one frozen span model, trained on $32^3$--$128^3$
-fields and used unchanged at $256^3$, reaches 2.20\,\% at 2.19$\times$
-the speed of Newton--BiCGStab at $256^3$, with settings chosen on 64
-validation cases; advection uses a precomputed quadratic tensor, and the
-nonlinear head did not generalise on this family and is not used.
-
-\paragraph{Navier--Stokes.}
-Here the head earns its place. On the held-out test cases the head with $k=\nNsHeadK$
-reaches 0.21\,\% at every mesh, at 0.93$\times$, 2.92$\times$ and
-8.01$\times$ the speed of CNAB2 at $32^3$, $64^3$ and $96^3$. The span of the same bank is
-33$\times$ less accurate at the same number of unknowns, and
-even the full rank-$\nNsBankR$ span reaches only 1.12\,\%:
-the moving vortices lie on a curved manifold that no small span contains. The error stays flat from
-$32^3$ to $96^3$ while the speedup rises.
-
-\paragraph{Where the speedup comes from.}
-On the linear problems the query does not iterate: it is one
-least-squares solve with a matrix factorised offline, and its time is
-mostly writing the $O(NR')$ output field (41.8 of 42.4\,ms on the Poisson
-development cases at $4096^2$, where CG needs about 5300 iterations over all nodes). On the
-nonlinear problems the iteration count does not grow with the mesh: about
-one Levenberg--Marquardt iteration per time step on Burgers and three
-Gauss--Newton sweeps on Navier--Stokes, each costing \eqref{eq:cost}
-(Table~\ref{tab:iterations}). The full-order solvers sweep all $N$ nodes
-in every iteration and, for CG, need more iterations as the mesh is
-refined \citep{Saad2003}. At the settings of Table~\ref{tab:headline},
-time grows as $N^{p}$ with $p\approx0.2$ for our model on Burgers and $0.3$
-on Navier--Stokes, against about $0.8$ and $0.9$ for the full-order solvers.
-
-\subsection{Comparison with Neural Operators and Other ROMs}\label{sec:results:neuralops}
-
-We next compare with the two alternatives a practitioner would consider
-instead: neural operators and other reduced-order models. We train an FNO \citep{Li2020FNO}, a U-Net \citep{Ronneberger2015UNet}, a
-DeepONet \citep{Lu2021DeepONet} and a Transolver \citep{WuTransolver2024}, widely used representatives of spectral, convolutional,
-branch--trunk and attention-based operators,
-on the evaluation mesh, on our model's training split (on Burgers, 128 trajectories of the
-same family) with a 3000\,s wall budget per network, and time them with the NM-ROM and the full-order solver in one
-allocation per problem (Table~\ref{tab:operators}).
+\begin{revblock}
+Against its own matched solver on the same mesh the fast setting is
+faster in 27 of the 30 cells of the submission's Table~1 (which include
+the box rows of Table~\ref{tab:boxes}); it is slower on Burgers 2D at
+$256^2$ and $512^2$ (0.58, 0.88$\times$) and Burgers 3D at $64^3$
+(0.93$\times$), and at Burgers 2D $4096^2$ it falls from 27.5 to
+13.9$\times$ (Figure~\ref{fig:matched}). On the original Navier--Stokes
+family a solver free to coarsen its mesh (RK4 on $32^3$--$48^3$) gives the
+head 0.63/1.65/0.94$\times$ at $32^3$/$64^3$/$96^3$, which is why we moved
+to the harder family: the advantage appears where a coarse mesh no longer
+suffices. Transform solvers are faster than our model on box Poisson 2D
+(0.14--5.05\,ms, 1.8--10$\times$ faster) and 3D and on heat 3D (fast
+setting at 0.50--0.61$\times$); our fast setting is 1.1--1.4$\times$
+ahead on heat 2D; on Burgers 2D a pseudo-spectral solver is faster up to
+$1024^2$ and slower from $2048^2$ (development cases). Box Poisson and heat
+are therefore correctness checks (Appendix~\ref{app:boxes}).
+\end{revblock}
