-\paragraph{Against neural operators, the NM-ROM wins as the mesh grows.}
-On small meshes the operators are competitive, since a forward pass is
-cheap: at $256^2$ on Burgers all four are faster than the NM-ROM, and at
-$32^3$ on Navier--Stokes the FNO is faster than our accurate setting and
-more accurate than our fast one. As the mesh grows, the NM-ROM's
-advantage grows with it: its query does not visit the grid until it
-returns the field, while every operator reads and writes the full grid,
-and the FNO, U-Net and Transolver errors on Burgers grow 2--5$\times$
-from $256^2$ to $2048^2$. From $1024^2$ on, and on Navier--Stokes from
-$64^3$, one NM-ROM setting is both more accurate and faster than all four
-operators (Table~\ref{tab:operators}, bold): on Burgers 8.42--9.04\,\%
-against 11.5--68.2\,\% for the operators, on heat 0.13\,\% against
-1.56--10.9\,\%, and on Navier--Stokes 0.21\,\% against 1.16--51.4\,\%.
-
-\paragraph{At the same number of unknowns, the learned manifold is far more accurate than prior ROMs.}
-With 16 unknowns, a linear basis and a quadratic manifold cannot follow
-the moving fronts of Burgers, and both run slower than the full-order
-solver; our head follows them at a cost that does not grow with the
-mesh. On the 64 test cases at $1024^2$ and $2048^2$, the head with
-$k=16$ reaches 8.42--9.04\,\% at 2.17--4.79$\times$ the speed of
-Newton--BiCGStab, against 34.1--41.7\,\% for POD-LSPG and a quadratic
-manifold \citep{GeelenWrightWillcox2022} (Table~\ref{tab:nmrom-baselines}).
-POD-LSPG approaches 1\,\% only with 256 or more unknowns, two orders of
-magnitude slower, and its basis is rebuilt from 128 new full-order
-trajectories at each mesh. A reproduced shallow masked-autoencoder
-NM-ROM \citep{KimChoi2022NMROM} did not reach a usable accuracy in our
-setting (145--175\,\%; Table~\ref{tab:nmrom-baselines}).
