-\subsection{Which Knob to Turn}\label{sec:results:tunability}\label{sec:results:best}
-
-A user of the trained model can turn two knobs without retraining: the
-bank width $R'$ and the choice between head and span (full ladders in
-Table~\ref{tab:tunability}). The width changes what the model can
-represent, so it is the accuracy knob. On Poisson at $4096^2$, narrowing the bank from
-$R'=512$ to $32$ raises the error 18.6$\times$ and moves the
-same model from 108$\times$ to 1136$\times$ the speed
-of CG on the test cases. On heat at $2048^2$ the span runs from 0.13\,\% at 216$\times$
-($R'=128$) to 1.08\,\% at 512$\times$ ($R'=32$), and on Burgers at
-$4096^2$ from 1.35\,\% at 10.7$\times$ ($R'=384$) to 8.24\,\% at
-44.5$\times$ ($R'=64$). The choice between head and span
-depends on the problem: on the linear problems the span is both more
-accurate and cheaper (\S\ref{sec:results:operators}), while on
-Navier--Stokes the head is the accurate end, 33$\times$ more
-accurate than the span at the same number of unknowns for about twice the
-cost.
+\begin{revblock}
+Against the CROM-style model, the closest relative (a neural field evolved
+by the equations online), our head is 4--5.5 times more accurate in the
+worst case (its median error is higher, 0.67 against 0.43\,\%) and
+16--87 times faster (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}): the space/latent
+factorisation removes repeated differentiation of the spatial network.
+Against the CoLoRA-style regression the result is a trade-off: our median
+error is four times lower at slightly lower cost, and its worst-case error
+is 3.6 times lower. With matched data, our median error is 7--9 times
+lower than FNO's and U-Net's and below the full-order solver's own median
+error against the reference; the operators have the lower worst-case error
+(0.43--0.51\,\% against 0.875\,\%). We compare these direct predictors
+by error, separately from the timing comparison with numerical solvers and ROMs. This replaces the submission's operator claim, which
+rested on 128 training trajectories (old comparison in
+Table~\ref{tab:operators}, whose heat rows, trained for only 6--7 epochs,
+support no claim). At $1024^2$ without retraining our model gives
+1.25\,\%, a zero-shot FNO 2.9--3.2\,\% and the U-Net about 53\,\%. On the harder Navier--Stokes family an FNO
+gives 2.41--2.52\,\% worst-case error against 0.90--1.39\,\%
+for our coordinate bank with the head. On the cylinder's standard window the best operator (an FNO,
+180 minutes of final training) also meets all six targets, at
+$9.9{\times}10^{-3}$ at Reynolds 105, where our width
+32 reaches $1.9{\times}10^{-3}$ with a fine step and width 24 meets all
+six at $5.5{\times}10^{-3}$; over the long window and outside the
+training band no operator meets all six (Appendix~\ref{app:operators}).
+\end{revblock}
